    <p>
        Note: This is similar to Problem 5 from Section 4.1 in your text.
    </p>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                A trough (like one that feeds domestic animals) is constructed
                by bending a <m>{4' \times 18'}</m> rectangular sheet of metal (that is, 4
                feet by 18 feet).
                Two symmetric folds 2 feet apart are made parallel to the
                longest side of the rectangle so that the trough has
                cross-sections in the shape of a trapezoid, as pictured below.
                (This is the cross-sectional area that comes from bending the 
                4 foot side, the trough in this picture
                goes back 18 feet into the page.)
            </p>
                <image width="45%">
      <shortdescription>
        A cross-section of the trough.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-hw9-a">
        <diagram dimensions="(350,100)" margins="18">
          <coordinates bbox="(-.5,-.1,4,0.8)">
            <line endpoints="((1,0),(3,0))" thickness="5"/>
            <line endpoints="((0.51,0.7),(1,0))" thickness="5"/>
            <line endpoints="((3.51,0.7),(3,0))" thickness="5"/>
            <line endpoints="((3.51,0.7),(0.51,0.7))" thickness="1" dash="4 4"/>
            <line endpoints="((0,0),(4,0))" thickness="1" dash="4 4"/>
            <label anchor="(2,-0.1)"><m>2</m></label>
            <label anchor="(0.75,0.1)"><m>\theta</m></label>
            <label anchor="(3.4,.3)"><m>1</m></label>
            <label anchor="(0.5,.4)"><m>1</m></label>

          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>

        </introduction>


        <task workspace="0in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                   Start by looking at the following triangle which corresponds to 
                   the left edge of the trough.  Label the 3 missing angles (in degrees!) and the 2 missing sides. 
                   All of your answers will depend on <m>\theta</m>.  (Make it very clear what your sides and angles are,
                   and what each value you found corresponds to on the picture.)

                </p>
                  <image width="45%" margins="0% 55%">
      <shortdescription>
        The left side of the trough which forms a right triangle.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-hw9-b">
        <diagram dimensions="(200,150)" margins="18">
          <coordinates bbox="(-.1,-.1,1.2,0.8)">
            <line endpoints="((0.51,0.7),(1,0))" thickness="5"/>
            <line endpoints="((1,0.7),(0.51,0.7))" thickness="1" dash="4 4"/>
            <line endpoints="((0,0),(4,0))" thickness="1" dash="4 4"/>
            <line endpoints="((1,0.7),(1,0))" thickness="1" dash="4 4"/>

            <label anchor="(0.75,0.1)"><m>\theta</m></label>
            <label anchor="(3.4,.3)"><m>1</m></label>
            <label anchor="(0.6,.4)"><m>1</m></label>

          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Draw a picture that divides the cross-section of the trough into two right triangles and a rectangle that you think will help determine the volume of the tank.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The volume of the <q>rectangular part</q> of the trough will be
                                        <m>\ell\cdot w \cdot h</m> where <m>\ell</m> is the length/depth that goes back into the page, <m>w</m> is the
                                        width, and <m>h</m> is the height.
                                        Find the volume of the rectangular part.
                                        Your answer will depend on <m>\theta</m>!  Write a quick sentence to explain
                                        where you got your <m>\ell</m>, <m>w</m>, and <m>h</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The volume of each of the <q>triangular</q> parts are
                                        <m>\frac{1}{2}b\cdot h\cdot \ell</m> where <m>b</m> is the base of the triangle, <m>h</m> is the
                                        height of the triangle, and <m>\ell</m> is the length/depth of the triangular part that 
                                        goes back into the page.
                                        Find the volume of the triangular parts (your answer, will, surprise, depend on
                                        <m>\theta</m>). Write a quick sentence to explain
                                        where you got your <m>b</m>, <m>h</m>, and <m>\ell</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Determine a formula for <m>V(\theta)</m> the volume of the trough as a function
                            of <m>\theta</m>.
                            (Hint: Just add together your answers for c and d, making sure to take into
                            account both triangular parts.)
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              What is the volume of the trough when <m>\theta = 45^{\circ}</m>?
                            Make sure your calculator or Desmos is in degrees if you are using computing
                            technology!  Write a short sentence to tell me what you did.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Graph <m>V(\theta)</m> in Desmos.
                            You can type V(theta) = ...(your answer)...
                            Your boss wants to know what angle you would use to get the maximum volume of
                            the trough.
                            What would you tell them? (Complete sentences, include an answer and a justification
                            as if talking to your future boss who isn't as good at math as you are.)
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>
</worksheet>